<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article article-type="other" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Exp Med</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Experimental Medicine</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0022-1007</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-9538</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2212163</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Cover</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title/></title-group><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>2</day><month>3</month><year>1998</year></pub-date><volume>187</volume><issue>5</issue><fpage>cover</fpage><lpage>cover</lpage><copyright-year>1998</copyright-year></article-meta></front><body><p>A solitary multinucleated osteoclast is immunostained for the transcription factor microphthalmia (Mi). This protein, which  localizes to the nucleus, is essential for normal osteoclast development. Rats harboring a deletion in Mi display severe osteopetrosis that improves with age. See related article in this issue by Weilbaecher et al., <related-article related-article-type="article-reference" vol="187" page="775" id="N0x34e2940N0x39bac20" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="9480987" ext-link-type="pubmed">pp. 775&#x02013;785</related-article>.</p></body></article>